WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.827
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Sunday, October eighteenth.

2
00:00:04.827 --> 00:00:12.334
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:16.285
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:16.285 --> 00:00:17.637
We start with Saudi Arabia and Yemen.

5
00:00:17.637 --> 00:00:25.255
The State Department travel account reminds US citizens in Saudi Arabia to follow national alerts from Saudi Civil Defense sent by cellular broadcast.

6
00:00:25.255 --> 00:00:32.960
It also says US government employees in the country now need special authorization from the Chief of Mission to use any Saudi airport.

7
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:40.041
The UK Foreign Office updated its Saudi Arabia safety and security advice to reflect the latest regional tensions.

8
00:00:40.041 --> 00:00:54.130
In Yemen, the Institute for the Study of War says government-aligned forces appear to have repelled Houthi efforts to take positions on the western outskirts of Taiz City, and that the Houthis targeted government weapons depots near Aden International Airport.

9
00:00:54.130 --> 00:01:00.041
ACLED says the Houthis have encircled Taiz, while government forces are making some gains in Marib.

10
00:01:00.041 --> 00:01:10.308
The Institute adds that government forces must hold ground in Waziyah and Madaribah districts to protect the recently recaptured Dhubab Airport from Houthi attempts to outflank them.

11
00:01:10.308 --> 00:01:22.109
In the Strait of Hormuz, Radio Free Europe reports Iran's Revolutionary Guards said a supertanker carrying more than two million barrels of crude oil exploded and caught fire after hitting a naval mine.

12
00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:26.994
The Institute for the Study of War notes an uptick in attacks on vessels near the strait.

13
00:01:26.994 --> 00:01:40.468
On the Pakistan and Afghanistan border, the Pakistani newspaper Dawn, citing state television, reports the army responded to what it called unprovoked firing with heavy weapons, destroying several Afghan Taliban posts.

14
00:01:40.468 --> 00:01:51.504
Global Decipher reports the clashes began in the Upper Kurram area of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and spread to border sectors in North and South Waziristan, with casualties reported on both sides.

15
00:01:51.504 --> 00:01:56.598
In Kenya, BBC Africa reports four new cases of Bundibugyo Ebola, bringing the total to seven.

16
00:01:56.598 --> 00:02:02.409
Three of the new patients are health workers who treated the country's first case, and the fourth is a relative.

17
00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:04.078
These are the first known infections passed on inside Kenya.

18
00:02:04.078 --> 00:02:13.552
The health ministry says there is no evidence of community transmission and the risk to the public is low.

19
00:02:13.552 --> 00:02:26.964
In Ukraine, Radio Free Europe reports Russia launched a major overnight barrage of ballistic and cruise missiles and more than three hundred drones, killing civilians, damaging apartment buildings and striking energy infrastructure.

20
00:02:26.964 --> 00:02:33.749
Officials in both Ukraine and Russia also reported civilian deaths after further drone attacks overnight into Sunday.

21
00:02:33.749 --> 00:02:47.516
Separately, Radio Free Europe and the Institute for the Study of War report President Trump signed an executive order on the night of October sixteenth waiving nearly all substantive sanctions, tariffs and restrictions in a bipartisan law aimed at pressuring Russia to end the war.

22
00:02:47.516 --> 00:02:54.418
The Institute assesses the decision will help Russia finance and prolong the war.

23
00:02:54.418 --> 00:02:56.648
Now a few quick notes by region.

24
00:02:56.648 --> 00:03:05.949
In the Middle East and North Africa, the International Committee of the Red Cross supported the release of one hundred fifty nine people detained in connection with the conflict in Yemen.

25
00:03:05.949 --> 00:03:12.871
Long War Journal reports Lebanon revoked an Iranian bank's license amid US pressure over Hezbollah financing.

26
00:03:12.871 --> 00:03:24.787
In Africa, BBC Africa reports a pastor and his wife were arrested in South Africa after two hundred people, some in chains, were found held at a suspected illegal rehabilitation centre.

27
00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:32.475
In South Asia, Dawn reports Pakistani security forces say they killed five militants who tried to set up a roadblock near Quetta, in Balochistan.

28
00:03:32.475 --> 00:03:40.583
In Asia Pacific, Canada raised its advice for Guam to avoid non-essential travel because of Typhoon Champi.

29
00:03:40.583 --> 00:03:49.671
In Europe, the Institute for the Study of War reports Polish authorities warn that Russia may escalate sabotage attempts against Polish logistics infrastructure.

30
00:03:49.671 --> 00:03:51.134
That's today's brief.

31
00:03:51.134 --> 00:03:59.425
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

32
00:03:59.425 --> 00:04:07.726
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

33
00:04:07.726 --> 00:04:09.863
This brief is digitally narrated.

34
00:04:09.863 --> 00:04:10.833
Questions?

35
00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:14.377
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

36
00:04:14.377 --> 00:04:16.368
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
